<DocsLayout title="Embedded UDFs" description="Register scalar, aggregate, and table UDFs through the embedded Zig API." headings={headings}>
  <PageHeader eyebrow="Extensibility" title="Embedded UDFs" description="Applications embedding thinDB can register functions directly, avoiding dynamic loading while retaining typed vector and table interfaces." />
  <h2 id="table-sdk">Typed table-function SDK</h2>
  <CodeBlock language="zig" code={`const thindb = @import("thindb");
const tdb = thindb.tdb;

const running_total = struct {
    pub const spec = tdb.TableFnSpec{
        .name = "running_total",
        .execution = .either,
    };
    pub const Input = struct { id: i64, customer_id: i64, amount: i64 };
    pub const Output = struct { id: i64, customer_id: i64, running: i64, normalized_amount: f64 };

    pub fn process(ctx: *tdb.Ctx, p: tdb.Partition(Input), out: *tdb.Writer(Output)) !void {
        _ = ctx;
        const ids = p.col(.id);
        const amounts = p.col(.amount);
        var total: i64 = 0;
        for (0..p.len) |i| {
            total += amounts[i];
            try out.row(.{
                .id = ids[i],
                .customer_id = p.key(.customer_id),
                .running = total,
                .normalized_amount = @floatFromInt(amounts[i]),
            });
        }
    }
};

try db.registerTableFn(running_total);`} />
  <p>Plain Zig struct fields define SQL column names and order. Mappings include <code>i32</code> to <code>INT</code>, <code>i64</code> to <code>BIGINT</code>, <code>f64</code> to <code>DOUBLE</code>, <code>[]const u8</code> to string, <code>tdb.Date</code>, <code>tdb.DateTime</code>, and <code>?T</code> for nullable columns.</p>
  <p><code>Partition</code> supports zero-copy column access with <code>col</code>, row access with <code>at</code>/<code>iter</code>, and partition keys with <code>key</code>. <code>Writer.row</code> makes missing, extra, or mistyped output fields compile errors.</p>

  <h3 id="table-patterns">Core table API patterns</h3>
  <CodeBlock language="zig" code={`pub fn process(
    ctx: *tdb.Ctx,
    p: tdb.Partition(Input),
    out: *tdb.Writer(Output),
) !void {
    // col() is the zero-copy columnar hot path.
    const amounts = p.col(.amount);
    const customer_id = p.key(.customer_id);

    // arena is query-lifetime scratch memory.
    const normalized = try ctx.arena.alloc(f64, p.len);
    for (0..p.len) |i| normalized[i] = @floatFromInt(amounts[i]);

    // iter() and at() are typed row-convenience paths.
    var rows = p.iter();
    var i: usize = 0;
    var total: i64 = 0;
    while (rows.next()) |row| : (i += 1) {
        total += p.at(i).amount;
        try out.row(.{
            .id = row.id,
            .customer_id = customer_id,
            .running = total,
            .normalized_amount = normalized[i],
        });
    }
}`} />
  <p>Use <code>Args</code> on the function struct for scalar configuration values supplied after the input subquery, and <code>Input2</code> for a second co-grouped input relation. Execution mode declares whether the function requires one global partition, accepts independent partitions, or supports either. The <a href={withBase('/functions/zig/')}>Zig functions guide</a> covers source submission, scalar <code>Args</code>, multiple inputs, SQL call syntax, and prebuilt libraries.</p>

  <h2 id="scalar-udfs">Scalar UDFs</h2>
  <Callout type="note" title="Embedded registration only"><p>Native scalar Zig UDFs are registered by the embedding application. <code>CREATE FUNCTION ... LANGUAGE zig</code> currently compiles table functions, not scalar kernels, and thinDB does not yet provide SQL DDL for scalar UDFs.</p></Callout>
  <CodeBlock language="zig" title="Kernel and registration" code={`fn scoreBucketKernel(
    ctx: *const thindb.udf.ScalarContext,
    args: []const thindb.storage.ColumnView,
    out: *thindb.engine.ColumnStore,
    row_count: usize,
) !void {
    const scores = args[0].data.double;
    var i: usize = 0;
    while (i < row_count) : (i += 1) {
        const bucket: i32 = if (scores[i] < 3.0) 0 else 1;
        try out.data.int.append(ctx.allocator, bucket);
    }
}

try db.registerScalarUdf(.{
    .name = "score_bucket",
    .arg_types = &.{ .double },
    .return_type = .int,
    .null_strategy = .propagates,
    .volatility = .immutable,
    .kernel = scoreBucketKernel,
});`} />
  <CodeBlock language="sql" title="Call from SQL" code={`SELECT event_id, score_bucket(score) AS bucket
FROM events
WHERE score_bucket(score) = 1;`} />
  <p>After registration, the name resolves like a built-in scalar function. Overloads may share a name when their argument type signatures differ.</p>

  <h3 id="scalar-kernel">Kernel contract</h3>
  <ul><li><code>args</code> contains one <code>ColumnView</code> per declared argument, in declaration order, with <code>row_count</code> aligned rows.</li><li>Read the union field matching each declared argument type, such as <code>data.double</code>, <code>data.int</code>, or <code>data.string</code>.</li><li>Append exactly <code>row_count</code> physical values to the output data field matching <code>return_type</code>.</li><li>Use <code>ctx.allocator</code> for required output or scratch allocation, but keep allocation out of the per-row loop.</li><li><code>ctx.user_data</code> exposes the optional application pointer from the registration descriptor. Its lifetime and synchronization are owned by the application.</li></ul>
  <CodeBlock language="zig" title="String output pattern" code={`const values = args[0].data.string;
var i: usize = 0;
while (i < row_count) : (i += 1) {
    const value = values.rowBytes(i);
    try out.data.string.appendValue(ctx.allocator, value);
}`} />

  <h3 id="scalar-nulls">Null handling</h3>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Strategy</th><th>Engine behavior</th><th>Kernel responsibility</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>.propagates</code></td><td>The result is null when any argument is null.</td><td>Append one physical placeholder/result per row; the engine writes output validity.</td></tr><tr><td><code>.absorbs</code></td><td>The default result validity is valid when at least one argument is valid.</td><td>Inspect argument validity and implement value semantics such as fallback/coalescing.</td></tr><tr><td><code>.kernel_managed</code></td><td>The engine does not define result nullability.</td><td>Append every physical value and write every output validity bit.</td></tr></tbody></table>
  <CodeBlock language="zig" title="Kernel-managed validity" code={`const base = out.rowCount();
const values = args[0].data.double;
var i: usize = 0;
while (i < row_count) : (i += 1) {
    const valid = args[0].isValid(i);
    try out.data.int.append(ctx.allocator, if (valid and values[i] >= 0) 1 else 0);
    try out.appendValidBit(ctx.allocator, base + i, valid);
}`} />
  <p>Volatility describes repeatability: <code>.immutable</code> depends only on its arguments, <code>.stable</code> is constant within a statement, and <code>.@&quot;volatile&quot;</code> may change for every evaluation. Declare the strictest truthful level; it is part of the function's optimization contract.</p>

  <h2 id="aggregate-udfs">Aggregate UDFs</h2>
  <CodeBlock language="zig" code={`try db.registerAggregateUdf(.{
    .name = "weighted_avg",
    .arg_types = &.{ .double, .double },
    .return_type = .double,
    .state_size = @sizeOf(WeightedState),
    .state_align = @alignOf(WeightedState),
    .init = weightedInit,
    .update_one = weightedUpdate,
    .update_batch = weightedUpdateBatch,
    .combine = weightedCombine,
    .finalize = weightedFinalize,
});`} />
  <p>Aggregate UDFs define a fixed-size state plus initialization, update, optional batch update, optional parallel combine, finalize, and optional destroy callbacks. A combine callback allows safe partial aggregation across workers.</p>

  <h2 id="contracts">Registration contracts</h2>
  <ul><li>Function names contain only alphanumeric characters and underscores.</li><li>Built-in scalar and aggregate names are reserved.</li><li>Scalar and aggregate functions may overload a user-defined name by argument signature, but duplicate signatures are rejected.</li><li>Arguments are capped at 16 for scalar and aggregate UDFs.</li><li>Descriptors and callback pointers must remain valid for the database/catalog lifetime.</li><li>Callbacks run inside the query process and must be thread-safe when parallel execution can reach them.</li></ul>
  <Callout type="note" title="Raw table API"><p><code>registerTableUdf</code> accepts a raw descriptor for advanced integrations. Prefer <code>registerTableFn</code>; its comptime reflection removes most schema and rectangular-output mistakes.</p></Callout>
